        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nAll sections in 'mini.statusline' are plain functions which return strings. It is left to user to combine them however they want. [Here is how it is done by default](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/78a4d457718060fe80480c72a7d64f6b23472d63/doc/mini-statusline.txt#L81-L98). For the most common case of combining groups, there is [`combine_groups()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/78a4d457718060fe80480c72a7d64f6b23472d63/doc/mini-statusline.txt#L161) which is a bit smarter version of plain string concatenation. Outside of that it is expected to manipulate section strings manually.\r\n\r\nMain reason for this kind of design is that it (like like it) would require almost as much code to configure inside `setup()` as to implement manually inside function. That is assuming configuration is kept flexible enough to allow different separators for different consecutive sections/groups.\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
